  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>NEET_UG_BIOLOGY_THELIVINGWORLD_MCQ_Q01 - Hierarchical arrangement of taxonomic categories</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Observe the given diagram representing the hierarchical arrangement of taxonomic categories in ascending order.</p><p><img src="@@PLUGINFILE@@/img-3.jpeg" alt="Taxonomic categories showing hierarchical arrangement in ascending order from Species to Kingdom" /></p><p>As we move upward in the direction of the arrows from Species to Kingdom, which of the following trends is observed?</p>]]></text>
      <file name="img-3.jpeg" encoding="base64"></file>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Step 1: Conceptual Principle</strong><br/>In the taxonomic hierarchy, organisms are classified into a sequence of ranks (taxa) arranged in ascending order from the lowest category, Species, to the highest category, Kingdom.</p><p><strong>Step 2: Evaluation of Common Characteristics</strong><br/>Lower taxa consist of closely related organisms that share a large number of fundamental similarities. As we move upward from Species to Kingdom, each successive category encompasses a broader group of organisms, so the number of common characteristics shared by members within the taxon decreases.</p><p><strong>Step 3: Evaluation of Classification Complexity</strong><br/>Because higher categories share fewer common features, determining the relationship of a taxon to other taxa at the same hierarchical level becomes increasingly difficult, making the problem of classification more complex.</p><p><strong>Step 4: Final Conclusion</strong><br/>Moving upward from Species to Kingdom results in a decrease in the number of common characteristics among members within a taxon and an increase in the difficulty of determining relationships to other taxa at the same level.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>4.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.2500000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The number of common characteristics among members within a taxon increases, and determining relationships to other taxa at the same level becomes simpler.</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The number of common characteristics among members within a taxon decreases, and determining relationships to other taxa at the same level becomes more complex.</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Both the number of common characteristics among members and the difficulty of determining relationships to other taxa at the same level decrease.</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The number of common characteristics remains constant across all ranks, while only the total number of species increases.</p>]]></text></answer>
